			<div class="content" id="post-content-4795368">
									

<p>Hello hello, responding to PM request.</p>
<p><strong>This review is now complete.</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Until talks with the United States Government are completed</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No need to capitalize government.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any inhabitants of SCP-XXXX caught leaving the designated exclusion zone are to be detained and used as bargaining chips for negotiations with the UIU.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems pretty on-the-nose for hostage-taking.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They are to be fed once a week and given 3Lbs1 of raw meat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd stick with metric. Every scientific organization in the world prefers it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-4 is to be contained in a larger equivalent of a standard humanoid containment unit, with food portions increased appropriately to its size.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think &quot;equivalent&quot; is the word you're looking for. There can be several standard sizes for containment chambers, and if this thing requires a bigger one, I think it's safe to assume they'd figure it out without needing to be told.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event more SCP-XXXX instances are identified, Foundation Agents</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The word &quot;agents&quot; doesn't need capitalization.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">At which point,</span> MTF-Epsilon-6 will transport it to a temporary containment location at the former NTS</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The town has a population of approximately 6<strong>,</strong>000 people</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The cult is currently engaged in hostilities with the United States Government and the UIU [SEE ADDENDUM XXXX-A]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is missing punctuation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1 through 5 is a small collection of Scarkic creatures</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Scarkic probably isn't what you meant.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>DNA testing of the entities has found the entities to be heavily deformed and mutated human DNA, though, SCP-XXXX-5 closely resembles non-anomalous human DNA with only minor alterations.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is awkward. Try reading it aloud.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Though higher caliber rounds have been reported to be able to penetrate the entity's exoskeleton.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not a complete sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Its talons and related appendages are found to be easily capable of slicing through the flesh and bone of an average human, but cannot penetrate Kevlar based body armour.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Average human&quot; implies that there are humans who would be resistant to a talon wound, which I doubt. Also, regular Kevlar does not protect against blades. It's a cloth, and knives can cut cloth. It's designed to protect against bullets and impact damage. Regular metal or ceramic armor would likely be a better choice.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The entity was given to the Foundation as a token of good faith by the U.S. Government.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, &quot;government&quot; doesn't need to be capitalized in situations like this. I'll leave it up to you to track the rest of these down.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it frequently makes writing motions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems like a lazy excuse to provide the thing with paper. What would this even look like? There's got to be a better way.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>when humans make physical contact with SCP-XXXX-2 they are absorbed by its Biomass.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Biomass doesn't need to be capitalized.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Partially translated documents by SCP-XXXX-2 seem to cooperate this as the information is, what would widely be considered, useful military intelligence such as Battleplans, division sizes, etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Cooperate&quot; is not the right word. Also, I can't really figure out what this sentence is trying to say. Read it aloud.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Entity located within an abandoned Gas Station along Highway ██ after a serious of disappearances were reported. The disappearances were traced back to the Gas Station and Agent ██████ contacted MTF-Epsilon-6 who recovered and moved the entity without incident.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Gas Station&quot; has no reason to be capitalized. Also, either stick with truncated sentences or complete sentences (&quot;The entity&quot;). Don't mix and match too much in formal writing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The entity has been observed running at speeds of 12mph over a distance of 100m when its acid sack is empty.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Stick with metric.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-3 possess slightly above average eyesight, roughly 20/30 vision.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I believe 20/30 vision would be worse than average.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>local foundation agents searched the Mojave around the reported area.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This time, you DO need to capitalize something&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with pale what like skin.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-4 has been seen to possess immense strength as expected of its size. With eyewitnesses claiming it is able to easily lift up and throw a standard-issue Chevrolet G506 truck 7 meters but is only able to flip over an M4 Sherman. The entity has also been observed to possess a healing factor, with standard gunfire repairing within two hours, but more severe injuries taking months if not years.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This reads like a superhero description rather than a scientific one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-4 was located a year after Incident-XXXX-A, local police reports were intercepted of a large creature attacking groups of hikers and other civilians.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence needs to be broken up.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-5 resembles a caucasian homo sapien of East Siberian decent with the physical appearance of a 30-year-old.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence needs some capitalization and italics.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The entity is 5ft8 tall</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Metric.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>brown hair with small signs of ageing present</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Aging.&quot; Also, brown with gray? Thinning brown? Signs of aging is not very descriptive.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Its behaviour is normal for a follower of Sarkicism,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't know what this means.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>he requires slightly reduced</p>
</blockquote>
<p>He or it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Though a few anomalous features were observed, such as the presence of SK-BIO Type 006 organisms within its body, which in turn contributed to the entity's longevity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Read this out loud.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On April 25th, 19██ SCP-XXXX-5, while being escorted to its monthly medical check-up, collapsed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Awkward wording.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Further examination found that the entity's recorded radiation sickness had progressed further despite the efforts of Foundation medical personnel, and SK-BIO Type 005 entities present within SCP-XXXX-5's body.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would cut the second part of this sentence. I think at this point we can assume that this thing was sick despite the entity already, since it was found that way.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Scarkic cult which would go on to overtake Arlingpool</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sarkic?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>they offered transportation to Mexico in order to escape what they saw as 'Eurasian Mekhanite influence'.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is offered the right word here?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the Church travelled north to modern-day Nye County, Nevada and settled into the ruins of Arlingpool.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm confused as to why Arlingpool is a ruin.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The town of Arlingpool itself was founded on 18th July 1850</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would recommend tracking down all the dates in this piece and checking, because they appear to have inconsistent formatting.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By 1879, the town was entirely depopulated.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So I guess that's why it's a ruin. Probably should know how this went earlier.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>locate sources of Fresh Water</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is this capitalized?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>when a squad of U.S. Army Riflemen on patrol stumbled upon the town by chance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What would US Army soldiers be doing patrolling the continental US in such a way that would allow them to &quot;stumble upon&quot; a US town?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with only one of them managing to escape. The sole survivor was given over to the UIU before the Foundation could respond.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So was the sole survivor the escapee? They found it? Also, using UIU without elaboration is going to leave out anyone who has no idea what that is.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following this encounter<strong>,</strong> the United States government, having gotten information about the feats the Church of the Scorched Earth were capable of performing, sent an envoy to meet with the Church.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I showed you where a comma should be, but in general, if your sentence has to have four commas in formal writing, you should restructure the sentence to try to eliminate it. Even less formal sentences like the previous one I just wrote can sound pretty run-on with even three commas.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>mutual-agreement of cooperation and trust</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Don't think you need a hyphen here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>irrelevant information has been expunged</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This should be its own sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Delegates and diplomats of the Church of the Scorched Earth and the United States of America laid before you is the proposed 'Treaty of Nevada'.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't feel like something that would be written into a treaty. Treaties tend to have exactly the language (and ONLY the language) that is necessary to explain the position of the people signing it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Article 1: The Government of the United States of America and her overseas territories will recognise the independence of the Church of the Scorched Earth and vice versa.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Really? &quot;Recognize&quot; in formal language usually means &quot;publicly recognize.&quot; Doesn't seem likely unless you're talking about a Broken Masquerade canon, which I don't think you are.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>vice verse.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>vice versa.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Article 5: As a sign of increased cooperation, the Church of the Scorched Earth is to provide it medical and agricultural expertise to the United States Government.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I know that this is supposed to depict the government handling stuff on their own without the Foundation knowing, but it seems pretty unusual to me that they'd be able to sign something that would give the US government access to potentially anomalous capabilities as a formal treaty without SOMEBODY from the Foundation finding out prior to this point. You've established that there are Foundation agents in local police precincts but not in the highest levels of US government that would know about this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>With at least three agents to be present at all times.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Another sentence fragment. And I doubt that a treaty would use UIU as an abbreviation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Below is an account from the diary of UIU Agent █████ Blake</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I see no reason for some of these redactions, like the first name of non-Foundation employee.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They have sections in the eastern parts of town, where the ground will be cried up Desert but then suddenly, futile soil<strong>,</strong> and farmland.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Dried? Also, desert doesn't need to be capitalized. The soil is probably fertile, not futile.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>ready to grab a First Aid kit,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First aid isn't capitalized.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But I suppose I should at least write about how us agents are doing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, I'm not really sure what this entire thing is. If it's a report, it doesn't read like one, especially not one from the 1930's. If it's an informal diary-type thing, then I'm not sure why this agent feels obligated to report on things their agency would specifically want to know like this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the other two being Agent ███████ Gordon &amp; Agent ███ Carr</p>
</blockquote>
<p>More redactions I don't think are necessary. Additionally, your tendency to use the ampersand for &quot;and&quot; makes them seem like a legal company or something. Weird to see in either formal or informal writing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A couple even spat at me and mumbled in whatever language it is they speak, no doubt saying insults.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A UIU agent sent to work with this population doesn't even know what language they're speaking?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The silver lining is, the kids seem fine with me. Somewhat anyway. They just sort of stare at me, not in a creepy way but a curious one. I went over to say hi and they either ran off or were ushered away by their parents. Only one of them let me get a word in, Vasily, he seems nice enough. Asks me about the world, I tell me what I can, though I feel the gaze of his dad whenever I do. Apparently his father is like the head surgeon or something along those lines. Apparently, he's part of a council of 'Karcists' who run this place.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, I think it's important to remember the time period you're supposed to be emulating here. This doesn't read like the typical diary or letter language from that time, it feels like a modern day blog post.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Well, I've certainly got my work cut out for me. I intend to send that request later in the week. Want to settle in first.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the sort of thing that feels weird to write in a diary, and one of the main reasons I try to warn people away from diaries, because diaries rarely mean &quot;real diaries&quot; and usually mean, &quot;excuse for me to have the character tell me everything I want to know.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>further improve relations between the SCP-XXXX-5 population</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The XXXX-5 population? -5 is an individual. Probably a better way to word this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The UIU agents continue to observe us, with little care for our customs.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Instead, he explained that while he does not agree with some aspects of our culture he understands these are millennia-old traditions and will not be changed by one man whose only been here for a few weeks. For that, he must be commended.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This all feels like exposition for the sake of the reader, not for the sake of the story.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He also took note of our distaste towards their 'communication devices'.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, this thing is alternating between casually using the term &quot;UIU&quot; and full understanding of what an &quot;agent&quot; is, but seems continually perplexed by the technology they're dealing with. You seem to have them one foot in the modern world, and one foot out, without committing to either side.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>he truly was a man of his world.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;&#8230;man of his word.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>proved to be vita for the country. The aid of SCP-XXXX helped advance discoveries to help the treatment of Malaria and Smallpox amongst other diseases.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;vital for the country.&quot; Also, I'm still a bit skeptical that the US government is rolling out technology from an anomalous group and is experiencing no pushback from an international organization whose mission statement is specifically to prevent stuff like this from happening. I understand that it's the &quot;point&quot; of the story, but we're not getting much of an idea of what the Foundation is doing right now or why they're just standing back and watching a cult give the US government stuff to inject into their own citizens with no real concern. Is the Foundation not talking to the government right now?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>after the attack on Pearl Harbour</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So since this is an American base with an American name, I would probably go with &quot;Pearl Harbor&quot;, as it's a proper name with that spelling. I think.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>to aid them in these campaigns.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What campaigns?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>they ultimately allowed this to take place as this was agreed upon in the Treaty of Nevada.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;this to take place as this&quot; is a little awkward.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite being assigned mostly to medical and other support roles, including the creation of SK-BIO organisms on the battlefield, by 1945 they were all listed as KIA.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So a few things about this.</p>
<p><strong>1)</strong> The creation of super-weapons on a battlefield is not medical. That's an incredibly unique and irreplaceable role. Saying they were &quot;mostly&quot; support while creating Sarkic monsters to fight for the US in a war is like saying the Avengers were just helping out fighting Thanos.<br />
<strong>2)</strong> In WW2, Navajo code talkers were so important that they were assigned their own bodyguards, as the code was THAT important to protect. If you had a group of wizards capable of creating monsters on a battlefield and were the only ones who could do it, the idea of them being all KIA seems like a story on its own, because these guys would have been enormously protected and I can't imagine they'd be anywhere near the front lines in a real-world scenario.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When I was 15, my high school sweetheart broke up with me. My heart shattered into a thousand pieces, I thought I could never feel worse. I was so wrong.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The idea of this guy comparing his high school love life to the eradication of an entire generation from a small, isolated society seems out of touch. He can express how awful this is for them without trying to relate with such a pithy personal experience that would in no way adequately compare.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I said to them, &quot;I have just received news of those of your children who were sent to the front.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You might want to explain at some point when this went from, &quot;These people are speaking in an unknown language&quot; to &quot;I know this unknown language perfectly.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>two days later every single carrier Pidgeon stationed there arrived at the designated arrival point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure how far back you need to go for &quot;pidgeon&quot; to be an acceptable spelling for pigeon, but either way, it shouldn't be capitalize. Also, using &quot;arrive&quot; twice in the same sentence is awkward.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So, agent, care to explain</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In this case, I would capitalized &quot;agent&quot; because it's being used as a term of address.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I…It was Christmas day. We'd been celebrating a merry Christmas, everyone had been drinking but me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, so I'm assuming a bunch of Sarkic cultists weren't celebrating Christmas. So what I'm to assume is that &quot;everyone&quot; refers to the entire UIU outpost, full of professionals, stationed in an area that is clearly tense due to the death of locals and expressed through hostile vandalism, and they decided to get very drunk in this situation, because it was Christmas? I'm pretty sure that even a regular military base stationed in a friendly country during a time of peace is never going to have every single member there completely smashed for any reason whatsoever.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Agent Stevenson: What do you mean silent?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What do you mean, &quot;What do you mean silent?&quot; That seems like a question designed to set up the poetic answer, not because it's a question that anyone would actually ask during a formal debriefing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Not who. What. The locals had sent these…creatures, two of them, they were covered in bones and had these sharp blades on their hands.14 Like something out of a horror comic. We'd seen them before, they were called 'Guardians', they kept the town safe.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, all of this makes me even more skeptical that the entire UIU group stationed there would have gotten blackout drunk while there were literal blade-handed monsters wandering around, even if they were used to them. Blade-monsters. Who lets their guard down around blade-monsters?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Alright, anything else to add before we end this interview?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't care when people &quot;wrap up&quot; their interviews like this after all the juicy stuff is out of the way. Having been a part of debriefs before, these things are detail-oriented and can last for hours even over routine subjects. It's okay to cut the transcribed portion of the interview out of a &quot;larger piece&quot; for an SCP article. Generally is more immersive than a two-minute interview.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>██rd Infantry Division</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm pretty sure there's no reason to black out the division number here if you're going to leave the &quot;rd&quot; on the end&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>all offers of Foundation aid were declined and the previous containment procedures were established.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, at a certain point, I'm not sure the Foundation would necessarily feel the need to defer to the government here. The goal of the Foundation is to prevent exactly this sort of thing from happening, and letting the government fumble around and risk a containment breach is not something I imagine the Foundation would tolerate without far more incentive than we're seeing here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Between the request for intervention and the arrival of the first military forces, the inhabitants of SCP-XXXX managed to expand their control to the border of the exclusion zone.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm just remembering that all the military-aged (and trained) individuals from this cult have been killed in action, so they're able to reassert this control using older villagers against a trained military.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>halted the Military's initial advance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Military doesn't need to be capitalized.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As the U.S. Army, even with UIU support and advisors, were initially unable to effectively combat the SK BIO Organisms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, so this is the same military that was USING these organisms in the war effort, seen what they can do on the battlefield, and still marched soldiers against them without having any idea how to defeat them? They watched these things tear up the battlefield and never got any clue what to do if they had to fight them?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We've been fighting these creatures for two weeks now. And…you know my brother James? When he came back from Europe, he described the kind of things the Krauts did…it's got nothing on this. I've been Goliaths tear trucks in half and flip over tanks. Men with swords for arms who would cut out a men's throat before they even got a chance to lift their rifles.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So&#8230; &quot;Dear Allison, I'm fighting secret war monsters that is probably one of the most closely guarded secrets that the military has, since they used them themselves, and this is a personal letter detail my adventures against them&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On the 20th January 1951, with the US military unable to make effective gains against SCP-XXXX,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Four years? After four years of trying to fight this isolated cult within US borders, knowing that fire and explosives work, and they still can't make ground? Why aren't they just dropping bombs on the village and turning it into a parking lot? Something that's vulnerable to both fire and explosives would be vulnerable to an aerial bombing, and it wouldn't have taken four years after a recent war that demonstrated the effectiveness of air power to figure that out.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Then you will face swift and utter destruction.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, from a government that has failed to figure out how to do that for four years.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>ordered all American Forces to withdraw.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Forces doesn't need to be capitalized.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Final thoughts:</p>
<p>This piece could definitely benefit from some narrowing of scope and formatting to clean up the pacing. I don't feel like it's necessary for the us to have specifics about the acid-spewing monsters or the blade-handed monsters or anything else that doesn't directly serve the story. We can know there are monsters, and I honestly think that the less we know about them, the better (especially since most of them were killed prior to the Foundation involvement). We don't need to know the specifics of an &quot;intelligence officer&quot; monster, especially since it doesn't really appear in your narrative.</p>
<p>I am also not a fan of everything being told in convenient diaries and informally-languaged reports. These are methods for expositing, and the storytelling itself can be pretty thin here. This is basically a violation of the &quot;show, don't tell&quot; storytelling advice. We're told a lot about what happens. &quot;Their throats were slit.&quot; &quot;We found grafitti.&quot; &quot;I am unhappy with these agents.&quot; Etc. It's hard to create a real sense of tension this way, and &quot;found diaries&quot; are so immensely commonplace on this site that it's hard to stand out using them.</p>
<p>Hope this helps, and best of luck in drafting and publishing.</p>

							</div>

								
