			<div class="content" id="post-content-4796419">
									

<p>Below are some clarifications and explanations to several points you've made.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Would&nbsp;you&nbsp;like&nbsp;to&nbsp;know&nbsp;more?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Yes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>This seems like a lazy excuse to provide the thing with paper. What would this even look like? There's got to be a better way.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Such as? Cause I can't think of one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also, using UIU without elaboration is going to leave out anyone who has no idea what that is.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The UIU is pretty well known within the Foundation fanbase, so I doubt they need much introduction. Plus if someone really doesn't know what they are then they can search them up. Also, why would the clinical Foundation provide a full explanation for every time their documents mention the UIU when the staff should know who they are.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I know that this is supposed to depict the government handling stuff on their own without the Foundation knowing, but it seems pretty unusual to me that they'd be able to sign something that would give the US government access to potentially anomalous capabilities as a formal treaty without SOMEBODY from the Foundation finding out prior to this point. You've established that there are Foundation agents in local police precincts but not in the highest levels of US government that would know about this?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you know what it's doing, then should it be good that it's doing that? Also, surely the Foundation doesn't know every single secret the US gov has. Plus this is the 1920's, the Foundation isn't quite as powerful as it is now.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A UIU agent sent to work with this population doesn't even know what language they're speaking?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well first of all who is going to teach it to them? None of the locals want to. And no outsider is going to know the language. Secondly, how many of those present at the negotiations with Native American tribes on the American side knew the language of those they were speaking with.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is the sort of thing that feels weird to write in a diary, and one of the main reasons I try to warn people away from diaries, because diaries rarely mean &quot;real diaries&quot; and usually mean, &quot;excuse for me to have the character tell me everything I want to know.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Any other suggestions would be welcome. Official reports would have no reason to mention a lot of this information and they would lack the emotion I'm trying to convey. So I don't see an alternative.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;vital for the country.&quot; Also, I'm still a bit sceptical that the US government is rolling out technology from an anomalous group and is experiencing no pushback from an international organization whose mission statement is specifically to prevent stuff like this from happening. I understand that it's the &quot;point&quot; of the story, but we're not getting much of an idea of what the Foundation is doing right now or why they're just standing back and watching a cult give the US government stuff to inject into their own citizens with no real concern. Is the Foundation not talking to the government right now?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have fixed this, thanks for pointing it out. Basically the Foundation is objecting and trying to intervene but the US gov is preventing them from doing so. Again, the Foundation is still quite young when compared to something like the US. They don't have the same level of power and influence they do today.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm pretty sure that even a regular military base stationed in a friendly country during a time of peace is never going to have every single member there completely smashed for any reason whatsoever.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They don't have every single member drinking. Like I said in the draft, Blake isn't drinking so he can keep watch. And I never said they were smashed, I just said they had been drinking. You can drink beer and not get drunk, it's easy.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>See, at a certain point, I'm not sure the Foundation would necessarily feel the need to defer to the government here. The goal of the Foundation is to prevent exactly this sort of thing from happening, and letting the government fumble around and risk a containment breach is not something I imagine the Foundation would tolerate without far more incentive than we're seeing here.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What exactly is the Foundation supposed to do? Show up to a heavily classified warzone, with uniforms and insignia that these soldiers have never seen and set up containment and hope the soldiers don't immediately call their superiors and ask about the new reinforcements? You have to remember while they are a global organisation the Foundation's major facilities are based in the US. What's their plan if the US decides that they don't want the Foundation in the borders anymore? Open the doors and let the anomalies out? Plus I'm sure the Foundation, at this point, gets at least some of its funding from national governments especially the US. The Foundation would face financial ruin. And before you say the US would not do this because it would risk destruction, I would like to point out the number of times the US genuinely planned to start a nuclear war rather than back down. Governments like the US will do crazy things when backed into a corner.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm just remembering that all the military-aged (and trained) individuals from this cult have been killed in action, so they're able to reassert this control using older villagers against a trained military.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's quite hard to fight something you don't know much about and uses both your dead and their own as spare parts.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They watched these things tear up the battlefield and never got any clue what to do if they had to fight them?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have made edits that address this</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dear Allison, I'm fighting secret war monsters that is probably one of the most closely guarded secrets that the military has, since they used them themselves, and this is a personal letter detail my adventures against them</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These soldiers have been told that they're dealing with a local uprising and that it is. For all they know this has reached national news. Again, this is the 1940's, it wasn't until the 50's that even half of the US had TVs. So the news didn't spread as fast. Plus, I wanted to mix things up from the use of diaries and there isn't really another choice here. But I am happy to hear any suggestions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why aren't they just dropping bombs on the village and turning it into a parking lot? Something that's vulnerable to both fire and explosives would be vulnerable to an aerial bombing, and it wouldn't have taken four years after a recent war that demonstrated the effectiveness of air power to figure that out.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I will admit I am quite stumped here. I want to force the US into deploying a nuclear weapon to show the reader why this kind of thing can't just be left to the government. But I am actually unsure of how to handle the air force question now that you mention it. Nothing short of creating a flying type SK Organism will fix that. But I am open to suggestions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I don't feel like it's necessary for the us to have specifics about the acid-spewing monsters or the blade-handed monsters or anything else that doesn't directly serve the story.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Are you honestly saying that the SCP foundation whose job is to contain and document weird stuff&#8230;shouldn't go into as much detail as possible about each of the SCP-XXXX organisms they have?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is basically a violation of the &quot;show, don't tell&quot; storytelling advice. We're told a lot about what happens.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, how else is it supposed to be told given the limitations of the format? How many actual people use 'show don't tell' in actual human conversation or documentations? I don't see any other way of conveying the information.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;found diaries&quot; are so immensely commonplace on this site that it's hard to stand out using them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Once again, I am happy to hear any alternatives because I don't see any.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>But I'd say the main thing is that not to sound rude but, this is nothing but negatives. Are you saying that there wasn't a single positive thing to say about the entire draft? I know you learn more from negative feedback, trust me I do. But you should still give at least some positive notes, otherwise, it makes me feel like the entire thing was a failure.</p>

							</div>

								
