			<div class="content" id="post-content-4797667">
									

<p>Responding to PM request.</p>
<hr />
<p>Big sorry about the delay. Life whisked me away for a bit there, but I have words for you.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So, I think the concept has some good footing for the most part. I was a little thrown off by how involved the U.S. government is in this whole thing, even going so far as using it for WWII, but I think it's an interesting story about how cruelty begets cruelty and the oppressiveness of those with great number and great firepower. Props for that.</p>
<p>However, I think there's some things here that need to be reworked. I agree with Francis in that there is quite a bit of irrelevant information here and there that distract from what you want to tell. The description itself could be <em>immensely</em> slimmed down, because I had a difficult time keeping interest in the statistics of these anomalies that never make an appearance in the Addendum. Frankly, it feels like I had wasted my time reading about SCP-XXXX-1 through -4 after reading the whole thing.</p>
<p>A few of the entries in the Addendum aren't the most necessary pieces of information. If I had to pick a few, I think the Callahan letter an the Agent Blake letter in Incident B could be scrapped, as they do nothing to move the story forward. I think the writing in the other entries could use a little tweaking here and there, personally; Daniel's reactions to things feels a bit performative (exaggerated? I can't find the right word, but it's hard to take him seriously at times) and Nathanael's obliviousness to upcoming technology is a bit eyerolling.</p>
<p>In the technical sense, I had a lot of issues with the spelling, punctuation, and grammar (SPaG) in this piece. The most common thing I saw was sentences that should have been compounded, like the example below.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation efforts to halt this trade and establish official containment were blocked by the US government. Who warned the Foundation against 'interfering in the internal affairs between the United States and her allies'.</p>
<p>Foundation efforts to halt this trade and establish official containment were blocked by the US <span style="color: blue">government, who</span> warned the Foundation against 'interfering in the internal affairs between the United States and her allies'.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The reason I'm not listing them all here is because, if I would have done so, I would have spent longer than two hours on this total and would have altogether taken more time to get this to you. I suggest looking over your work for any errors in SPaG.</p>
<p>In conclusion, it isn't quite there yet for me to say I'd upvote this. I like your concept and I like the way that it's presented for the most part. However, the things I have listed above do not make this a strong approval from me. I encourage you to take some more time with this draft, maybe even reach out to those that greenlit it, to make it the best that it can be.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
