			<div class="content" id="post-content-4800208">
									

<p>Thanks for the feedback, I have some responses to your points below.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Would&nbsp;you&nbsp;like&nbsp;to&nbsp;know&nbsp;more?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Yes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Frankly, it feels like I had wasted my time reading about SCP-XXXX-1 through -4 after reading the whole thing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While I can certainly remove some of the SCP-XXXX entities, I cannot remove them all. And the ones I do keep will probably retain the specifics of their capabilities. Why would an organisation as scientific and clinical as the Foundation not be very specific with its information? I thought the clinical tone was a good thing?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If I had to pick a few, I think the Callahan letter an the Agent Blake letter in Incident B could be scrapped, as they do nothing to move the story forward.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have to strongly disagree here. Callahan's letter informs the reader of the kind of horrors they had to face when fighting the SCP-XXXX inhabitants. And I like to think it does so in a far more engaging and humanistic way than if it was just statistics.</p>
<p>As for Blake's letter, that explores the last attempt by the US to resolve the crisis peacefully. IRL, governments will attempt to use diplomacy before resorting to the big guns. For example, the US requested Japan surrender BEFORE they decided to use nukes. So this letter shows that last effort from someone who was there, which to me at least seems very important.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Daniel's reactions to things feels a bit performative (exaggerated? I can't find the right word, but it's hard to take him seriously at times)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well considering how he's just seen a flesh worshipping cult, had to tell said cult all their sons they sent to war are dead, saw his friends get murdered by said cult before leaving and is about to be killed himself, I think he's allowed to be a bit overdramatic. But if you have any other suggestions for how he should react I'd be happy to hear it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nathanael's obliviousness to upcoming technology is a bit eyerolling.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Proto-Sarkic cults are incredibly isolationist and shun almost all technology. So Nathaniel would have no understanding of this technology, which means he's going to be oblivious to it. This cannot be changed without fundamentally changing the Sarkics and their belief system.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

