<blockquote>
<p>While I can certainly remove some of the SCP-XXXX entities, I cannot remove them all. And the ones I do keep will probably retain the specifics of their capabilities. Why would an organisation as scientific and clinical as the Foundation not be very specific with its information? I thought the clinical tone was a good thing?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because there are better ways to describe them rather than in great detail, and clinical tone isn't deep analysis of details. Clinical tone is brief and succinct. If you must describe them, I think describing them in the general would be the most apt. If I may provide an example (but surely not a replacement)&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is the designation provided for various humanoid lifeforms kept in Foundation captivity after their retrieval from Arlingpool, Nevada in 19XX. With the exception of SCP-XXXX-A, each possesses unique traits that are similar to Proto-Sarkic mutations, including the presence of SK-BIO organisms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>With something like this, it would be easy to discern that, yes, these are Proto-Sarkics and, yes, one of them is special in nature. The description in the SCP format serves to set the rest of the article in most narratives; characters, setting, a general overview of events, all of it is revealed within the description before getting to the specifics.</p>
<p>Now, to be clear, I don't read a lot of Sarkic articles (despite somehow critting a lot of them), so I don't know a lot. If Proto-Sarkics are isolationist, whatever. Makes enough sense with that being said.</p>
<p>This is all to say that those are the only two points that I'm willing to bring up. Everything else was stuff that was brought up by Francis that I also agreed with, and I'm starting to notice some pushback on those points. If you don't want to change it, that's fine. At the end of the day, it is your article and your narrative. However, I will be abstaining from any further crit responses on this draft.</p>

