<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hey there thesilentking. First off, allow me to apologise for the long delay in getting this review to you. Let’s take a look at your latest draft!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;LBL</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Special Containment Procedures:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The start of this is incredibly messy and hard to follow. Consider colour-coding your text, especially if you’re using updates, which are usually in blue.</p>
<p>Also, you can start the updated conprocs on a new paragraph.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>are to be detained and hand over</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Keep your tenses consistent. “hand” -&gt; “handed”.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with necessary alternations being made in accordance with standard containment of SK-BIO entities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does this mean? For a new reader, it doesn’t give me additional information that helps me understand the scip. Either specify what these “alternations” are or add a footnote to explain what “SK-BIO” refers to.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1, 3, and 4 are only released from their containment units</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is already getting complicated. I’m generally not a fan of multiple instance-labelling, as it can get difficult for the reader to follow, fast. Generally, ask yourself if the payoff is worth it, and whether you can describe the entities without resorting to these labels, especially if they’re referring to an object with similar qualities.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>NTS,1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Minor nitpick: your footnote should be before the comma, not after.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>forfilling specific combat roles</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Think you meant “fulfilling”.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In most respects SCP-XXXX-5 was a normal non-anomalous human.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Keep your tenses consistent. In the previous para, you were using “was” as “is”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-5 was located in a small wooden hut, guarded by SCP-XXXX-4.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, this is confusing if we weren’t able to remember your labelling system. One option is to simply get rid of xxxx-5 label and mention the Karcist by name. OR convert his label to a PoI-designation, since he’s a separate humanoid entity.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>To date, these have been the only SCP-XXXX entities to be recovered, though sightings of similar entities in the Bohemian and Bavarian Forests as well as in Central Italy have been reported. An investigation into a possible link between these entities and SCP-XXXX is currently ongoing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quite the hefty Description-segment. You’ve basically given away your entire narrative here, with testing logs, discovery logs and what I assume to be a final interview log with the Karcists all done and wrapped up.</p>
<p>One downside of this is you might tend to repeat all this later on in the article again, which is not productive. Usually, the Description segment is kept sparse, with the key details summarised and provided. Then, you’d go into detail in the latter addenda.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The following is a detailed timeline of events leading up to Incident XXXX-A &amp; B</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Notice I still don’t know what these two incidents are referring to. They’re briefly mentioned earlier in the article but you didn’t specify what they were.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Sarkic cult which would go on to overtake Arlingpool, according to oral histories recovered from SCP-XXXX-5, originated from the Amur region of Siberia. Then known as the Church of Oastrikyo, they were hunted and driven away by Tsarist forces in 181█3, forcing them to flee to the coast.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sorry, but what <em>is</em> this section supposed to be? Is it a log by the researcher? Uncovered documents? An interview with a Historian? I’m highly hesitant to read through a Wikipedia entry (basically an info-dump) as it’s likely to not contain any narrative or character development.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Below is an account from the diary of UIU Agent Daniel Blake5, from his first arrival at SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the first interesting part of the article, tbh. I always believe character should come first. Consider if you had simply moved this segment to the top of this section. Do you still need all that info when you can simply <em>show</em> what the town is like through the agent’s eyes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The military's intervention eventually grew into a stalemate, with the SCP-XXXX inhabitants able to prevent significant military advances with the SK BIO organisms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So I do like the fact you’ve interwoven historical events with the actions of the town and entities.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>(+/-) Overall, I’m torn. I do think the article picks up towards the end, but I’m still left wondering how the sarkic elements fit into this. It’s basically just producing monsters to fight in wars, yes? Eventually, this leads to conflict with the government (but not the Foundation?) I feel that the concept has potential, but your sarkic elements need to be emphasised a little more to really drive home the horror aspect.</p>
<p>(-) Structure and execution are where I’m struggling: the entire article reads like a history lesson, which keeps its clinical tone but lacks any tension-building or narrative stakes. You’re basically retelling the events in a discovery log, and by the end of the article, it’s just an “okay, that’s cool. So what?” kinda feeling. It doesn’t help that the Karcist dies at the very beginning of the article, so we kinda already know that the Foundation has prevailed.</p>
<p>You’d need to majorly restructure this, however, so that it’s more in line with a traditional narrative. If you’re playing with sarkicism, it’s essential to keep some of the mystery alive as we progress through the article. Make us wonder what’s going on instead of just telling us the facts from the start. Have the Foundation investigate the claims and forget the US government, I feel.</p>
<p>Overall, I would no-vote this in its current state.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>If you decide to revise the draft / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll do my best to take another look. For now, all the best with the writing!</p>

